# Reference versions and evidence provenance

Status: active. This clarifies the reference-version question in the repair
handoff; it does not change the behavioral contract or accept a release.

## Observed identities

On 2026-10-28, an agent-browser read of the source deck's live home page
returned the visible footer link `Decko v0.19.1`,
pointing to `http://decko.org/`. This verifies the live site's advertised
version. A footer is not a deployed dependency lock or a source revision:
exact installed gems and local modifications still require deployment
provenance. No sign-in, content edit or deployment was performed.

The existing test reference is separately pinned by
`reference-harness/browser/cucumber/app/Gemfile.lock`: `decko (0.20.0)`,
`card (1.110.0)` and `cardname (0.20.0)`. Earlier executed reference receipts
identify those versions too. The README already distinguishes historical
live-site 0.19.1 expectations from the 0.20.0 oracle. They are different
evidence sources and must remain distinguishable.

The handoff's 0.20.5 designation has not been established by a source or
runtime record. The retained official RubyGems version response contains
0.20.0 and 0.19.1 but no 0.20.5. That observation does not prove that an
unpublished variant cannot exist. It also does not make a remembered version
number a prerequisite for continued repair work.

## Decision and completion checks

Continue the general engine repairs and failing-reference diagnosis against
the existing 0.20.0/card 1.110.0 test reference, reporting that exact identity.
Treat the source deck's advertised 0.19.1 as a separate migration-source identity;
do not silently replace either reference or fold their results into one grade.
No dependency download or installation is required by this decision.

Resolve any exact live-runtime/source revision through deployment records or
read-only inspection under its access rules. The lead owns this investigation;
the operator is not required to identify a gem version. Keep issue #46 open
for that provenance and the cross-version compatibility map, rather than as
an operator approval gate on unrelated repairs.

The six failed reference sign-ins and mail-origin comparison differences
remain acceptance work. Their diagnosis does not depend on selecting an
unavailable 0.20.5 reference. Preserve the failed observations, obtain the
missing causal evidence, and regenerate affected comparisons only after the
mechanism is established. Existing 0.20.0 evidence proves no 0.19.1 migration
or 0.20.5 compatibility by itself.
